                    "id": "foundations",
                    "label": "Probability Foundations",
                    "content": "## Probability Foundations\n\n### Classical vs Axiomatic Definitions\n\n**Classical:** \\( P(A) = \\dfrac{\\text{favourable cases}}{\\text{total equally likely cases}} \\)\n\n**Axiomatic (Kolmogorov):** A probability measure \\(P\\) on sample space \\(S\\) satisfies:\n1. \\( P(A) \\ge 0 \\) for every event \\(A\\)\n2. \\( P(S) = 1 \\)\n3. For mutually exclusive \\(A_1, A_2, \\ldots\\): \\( P\\!\\left(\\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i\\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^\\infty P(A_i) \\)\n\n**Consequences:**\n- \\( P(A^c) = 1 - P(A) \\)\n- \\( P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B) \\) (Addition rule)\n- \\( P(\\emptyset) = 0 \\)\n- \\( A \\subseteq B \\Rightarrow P(A) \\le P(B) \\)\n\n---\n\n### Conditional Probability & Independence\n\n\\[\nP(A|B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}, \\quad P(B) > 0\n\\]\n\n**Multiplication rule:** \\( P(A \\cap B) = P(A|B)\\,P(B) = P(B|A)\\,P(A) \\)\n\n**Independence:** \\( A \\perp B \\iff P(A \\cap B) = P(A)\\,P(B) \\iff P(A|B) = P(A) \\)\n\n**Pairwise** independence does NOT imply **mutual** independence.\n\n---\n\n### Law of Total Probability\n\nIf \\(\\{B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_k\\}\\) is a **partition** of \\(S\\) (mutually exclusive & exhaustive):\n\\[\nP(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} P(A | B_i)\\, P(B_i)\n\\]\n\n### Bayes' Theorem\n\n\\[\nP(B_k | A) = \\frac{P(A | B_k)\\, P(B_k)}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k} P(A | B_i)\\, P(B_i)}\n\\]\n\n- \\(P(B_k)\\) = **prior** probability; \\(P(B_k|A)\\) = **posterior** probability\n- \\(P(A|B_k)\\) = **likelihood**\n\n---\n\n### Distribution Functions (CDF) — Properties\n\nFor a random variable \\(X\\), \\(F(x) = P(X \\le x)\\):\n\n1. **Non-decreasing:** \\(x_1 < x_2 \\Rightarrow F(x_1) \\le F(x_2)\\)\n2. **Right-continuous:** \\(F(x^+) = F(x)\\)\n3. **Limits:** \\(F(-\\infty) = 0\\), \\(F(+\\infty) = 1\\)\n4. **Jump = probability:** \\(P(X=x) = F(x) - F(x^-)\\)\n5. \\(P(a < X \\le b) = F(b) - F(a)\\)\n\n---\n\n### Moments\n\n- **Raw moments:** \\(\\mu'_r = E(X^r)\\)\n- **Central moments:** \\(\\mu_r = E(X-\\mu)^r\\), where \\(\\mu = \\mu'_1\\)\n- **Relations:**\n  - \\(\\mu_2 = \\mu'_2 - (\\mu'_1)^2\\)\n  - \\(\\mu_3 = \\mu'_3 - 3\\mu'_2\\mu'_1 + 2(\\mu'_1)^3\\)\n  - \\(\\mu_4 = \\mu'_4 - 4\\mu'_3\\mu'_1 + 6\\mu'_2(\\mu'_1)^2 - 3(\\mu'_1)^4\\)\n\n### Conditional Expectation\n\n\\[\nE(X) = E[E(X|Y)] \\quad \\text{(Law of Total Expectation)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Var}(X) = E[\\text{Var}(X|Y)] + \\text{Var}[E(X|Y)] \\quad \\text{(Eve's Law)}\n\\]\n\n> **ISS tip:** Bayes theorem with 3 hypotheses is a guaranteed MCQ. Know the CDF right-continuity property — it distinguishes \\(P(X \\le x)\\) from \\(P(X < x)\\). Eve's law (variance decomposition) appears regularly."
