            "tips": "**Sampling (Final Tips & Exam Strategy):**\n\n1. **Master the 4-Step Derivation Flow:** Since ISS is a descriptive exam, every core topic answer must follow a strict mathematical flow:\n  1. State the Estimator, \n  2. Prove Unbiasedness via Expectations, \n  3. Derive the Variance, \n  4. Derive the Unbiased Variance Estimator.\n2. **Standard Inequalities & Decompositions:** Comparative proofs are high-frequency questions. Practice the step-by-step derivations for \\(V_{opt} \\le V_{prop} \\le V_{SRS}\\) (using the sum of squares decomposition identity) and why Regression \\(\\le\\) Ratio.\n3. **Taylor Expansion for Approximations:** For Ratio and Product estimators, explicitly write the bivariate Taylor series expansion \\((1+e_x)^{-1}\\) to derive Bias and Variance up to \\(O(n^{-1})\\). Examiners actively look for these expansion steps.\n4. **High-Yield PPS Derivations:** Unequal probability sampling is heavily tested. Ensure you can independently derive the Des Raj and Das ordered/unordered estimators for \\(n=2\\), and prove the equivalence of the Horvitz-Thompson and Sen-Yates-Grundy variance forms.\n5. **Applied Official Statistics:** Do not skip the NSSO/NSO methodology. Mentioning the transition from Schedule 10 to the **PLFS rotational panel design**, or how NSO uses **IPNS** via separate field parties to measure enumerator bias, fetches premium marks over standard textbook answers.\n6. **Notational Consistency:** Stick rigidly to standard notations used in benchmark textbooks like W.G. Cochran (*Sampling Techniques*), Sukhatme & Sukhatme, or Parimal Mukhopadhyay. Unconventional notations often frustrate examiners."
