                    "id": "applied-ts-components-smoothing",
                    "label": "Time Series — Components, Trend & Smoothing",
                    "content": "## Time Series — Components, Models, Trend Determination & Smoothing\n\n### Components of a Time Series\n\nAn economic time series \\( Y_t \\) is decomposed into four components:\n\n1. **Trend (T):** Long-term underlying movement — secular growth or decline over many years.\n2. **Seasonal (S):** Regular periodic fluctuations with period \\( \\le 1 \\) year (quarterly, monthly).\n3. **Cyclical (C):** Oscillations with period \\( > 1 \\) year — business cycles (Prosperity → Recession → Depression → Recovery).\n4. **Irregular/Random (I):** Unpredictable, non-systematic residual variation.\n\n**Models:**\n- **Additive:** \\( Y_t = T_t + S_t + C_t + I_t \\) — assumes components are independent and on the same scale.\n- **Multiplicative:** \\( Y_t = T_t \\times S_t \\times C_t \\times I_t \\) — more appropriate when seasonal amplitude grows with the trend level.\n- **Mixed:** e.g., \\( Y_t = T_t \\times S_t + I_t \\)\n\n---\n\n### Tests for Trend and Seasonality\n\n**1. Turning Point Test (for randomness vs trend):**\nA **turning point** at time \\( t \\) occurs if \\( y_t > y_{t-1}, y_{t+1} \\) or \\( y_t < y_{t-1}, y_{t+1} \\).\n- Under randomness: \\( E(p) = \\dfrac{2(n-2)}{3} \\), \\( \\text{Var}(p) = \\dfrac{16n-29}{90} \\).\n- Test statistic: \\( z = \\dfrac{p - E(p)}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}(p)}} \\sim N(0,1) \\) for large \\( n \\).\n- Too few turning points → evidence of trend; too many → possible seasonality.\n\n**2. Kendall's Rank Correlation Test:**\nCompute Kendall's \\( \\tau \\) between \\( y_t \\) and time \\( t \\). Significant \\( \\tau > 0 \\) indicates monotonic upward trend.\n\n**3. ACF Test for Seasonality:**\nIf the sample ACF \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\) is significantly large at seasonal lags \\( k = s, 2s, 3s, \\ldots \\) (e.g., \\( k = 12, 24, \\ldots \\) for monthly data), seasonality is present.\n\n---\n\n### Determination of Trend\n\n**Method 1: Least Squares Method**\nFit a polynomial in \\( t \\) by minimising \\( \\sum(Y_t - \\hat{T}_t)^2 \\).\n\n*Linear trend:*\n\\[\n\\hat{T}_t = a + bt\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{\\sum t Y_t - n\\bar{t}\\bar{Y}}{\\sum t^2 - n\\bar{t}^2}, \\qquad a = \\bar{Y} - b\\bar{t}\n\\]\nIf time origin is centred (\\( \\sum t = 0 \\)): \\( b = \\dfrac{\\sum tY_t}{\\sum t^2} \\), \\( a = \\bar{Y} \\).\n\n*Quadratic trend:* \\( \\hat{T}_t = a + bt + ct^2 \\) — add \\( t^2 \\) as a regressor. Solve normal equations.\n\n*Exponential trend:* \\( T_t = ab^t \\); take logs: \\( \\log T_t = \\log a + t\\log b \\), then use linear LS.\n\n**Method 2: Moving Average Method**\nA centred \\( k \\)-period moving average:\n\\[\n\\hat{T}_t = \\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{j=-(k-1)/2}^{(k-1)/2} Y_{t+j} \\quad (k \\text{ odd})\n\\]\nFor even \\( k \\) (e.g., \\( k=4 \\) for quarterly): use a 2×4 centred MA (average of two overlapping 4-period MAs).\n\n*Advantage:* No assumption about functional form of trend.\n*Disadvantage:* Loses \\( (k-1)/2 \\) observations at each end; cannot forecast.\n\n**Method 3: Method of Semi-Averages**\nDivide the series into two equal halves; compute the mean of each half; the straight line through these two means is the trend line. Simple but crude.\n\n---\n\n### Measurement of Seasonal Variation\n\n**Method 1: Ratio-to-Trend (Percent of Trend):**\n1. Determine trend \\( \\hat{T}_t \\) by least squares or MA.\n2. Compute \\( S_t^* = (Y_t / \\hat{T}_t) \\times 100 \\) (multiplicative) or \\( Y_t - \\hat{T}_t \\) (additive).\n3. Average \\( S_t^* \\) for each season (January, February, … or Q1, Q2, …) across years.\n4. Normalise so seasonal indices sum to \\( 1200 \\) (monthly) or \\( 400 \\) (quarterly).\n\n**Method 2: Ratio-to-Moving-Average (most commonly used):**\n1. Compute centred 12-month (or 4-quarter) moving average \\( \\hat{T}_t \\) — this removes seasonality.\n2. Compute ratio \\( Y_t / \\hat{T}_t \\times 100 \\).\n3. Average ratios for each month/quarter across years → **seasonal indices** \\( S_i \\).\n4. Deseasonalised series: \\( Y_t^{\\text{adj}} = Y_t / S_i \\times 100 \\).\n\n**Deseasonalisation:** Divide (multiplicative) or subtract (additive) seasonal indices from original series. Deseasonalised data shows the combined trend + cyclical + irregular components.\n\n---\n\n### Measurement of Cyclical Fluctuations\n\n1. Remove trend: \\( TC_t = Y_t / \\hat{T}_t \\) (after removing seasonal with seasonal indices).\n2. Apply a short-term moving average to \\( TC_t \\) to eliminate irregular component → \\( \\hat{C}_t \\).\n3. Irregular component: \\( \\hat{I}_t = TC_t / \\hat{C}_t \\).\n\n---\n\n### Exponential Smoothing & Forecasting\n\n**Simple Exponential Smoothing (SES):**\nSuitable for stationary data (no trend, no seasonality).\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{t+1} = \\alpha Y_t + (1-\\alpha)\\hat{Y}_t, \\quad 0 < \\alpha < 1\n\\]\n\n**Recursive expansion:**\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{t+1} = \\alpha\\sum_{j=0}^{t-1}(1-\\alpha)^j Y_{t-j} + (1-\\alpha)^t Y_1\n\\]\nShows SES is an **exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA)** — recent observations get highest weight.\n\n- Small \\( \\alpha \\): slow adaptation, smooth forecasts.\n- Large \\( \\alpha \\): rapid adaptation, tracks data closely but noisy.\n\n**Holt's Double Exponential Smoothing (linear trend):**\n\\[\nL_t = \\alpha Y_t + (1-\\alpha)(L_{t-1} + T_{t-1})\n\\]\n\\[\nT_t = \\beta(L_t - L_{t-1}) + (1-\\beta)T_{t-1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{t+h} = L_t + hT_t\n\\]\n\n**Holt–Winters Method (trend + seasonality):**\n\n*Additive:*\n\\[\nL_t = \\alpha(Y_t - S_{t-p}) + (1-\\alpha)(L_{t-1}+T_{t-1})\n\\]\n\\[\nT_t = \\beta(L_t-L_{t-1}) + (1-\\beta)T_{t-1}\n\\]\n\\[\nS_t = \\gamma(Y_t - L_t) + (1-\\gamma)S_{t-p}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{t+h} = L_t + hT_t + S_{t+h-p}\n\\]\n\n*Multiplicative:*\n\\[\nL_t = \\alpha(Y_t/S_{t-p}) + (1-\\alpha)(L_{t-1}+T_{t-1})\n\\]\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{t+h} = (L_t + hT_t)\\times S_{t+h-p}\n\\]\nwhere \\( p \\) = seasonal period (12 for monthly, 4 for quarterly).\n\n> **ISS tip (Descriptive):** The methods of trend determination are all examinable: (1) Least squares — set up normal equations and solve; (2) Moving averages — specify the centring procedure for even \\( k \\); (3) Semi-averages — simple but state its limitations. For SES, derive the EWMA form recursively — this is a standard short-answer question. Seasonal indices must sum to 1200 (monthly) or 400 (quarterly) after normalisation — state this condition explicitly."
