                    "id": "applied-ts-arima-boxjenkins",
                    "label": "Stationary Processes & Box–Jenkins",
                    "content": "## Stationary Processes — AR, MA, ARMA, ARIMA & Box–Jenkins Methodology\n\n### Discrete Parameter Stochastic Process\n\nA time series \\( \\{Y_t\\} \\) is a discrete-time stochastic process.\n\n**Strict stationarity:** The joint distribution of \\( (Y_{t_1},\\ldots,Y_{t_k}) \\) equals that of \\( (Y_{t_1+h},\\ldots,Y_{t_k+h}) \\) for all \\( h \\) and \\( k \\).\n\n**Weak (covariance) stationarity:**\n1. \\( E(Y_t) = \\mu \\) (constant mean)\n2. \\( V(Y_t) = \\gamma_0 \\) (constant variance)\n3. \\( \\text{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-k}) = \\gamma_k \\) (depends only on lag \\( k \\))\n\n**ACVF:** \\( \\gamma_k = E[(Y_t-\\mu)(Y_{t-k}-\\mu)] \\). Properties: \\( \\gamma_0 > 0 \\), \\( \\gamma_k = \\gamma_{-k} \\), \\( |\\gamma_k| \\le \\gamma_0 \\).\n\n**ACF:** \\( \\rho_k = \\gamma_k/\\gamma_0 \\). Properties: \\( \\rho_0 = 1 \\), \\( \\rho_k = \\rho_{-k} \\), \\( |\\rho_k| \\le 1 \\).\n\n**PACF \\( \\phi_{kk} \\):** Partial autocorrelation at lag \\( k \\) — correlation between \\( Y_t \\) and \\( Y_{t-k} \\) after removing the linear effect of \\( Y_{t-1},\\ldots,Y_{t-k+1} \\). Obtained from the Yule–Walker equations.\n\n---\n\n### Backshift Operator\n\n\\( BY_t = Y_{t-1} \\), \\( B^k Y_t = Y_{t-k} \\), \\( \\Delta Y_t = (1-B)Y_t = Y_t - Y_{t-1} \\), \\( \\Delta^d = (1-B)^d \\).\n\n---\n\n### MA(q) Model\n\n\\[\nY_t = \\mu + \\varepsilon_t + \\theta_1\\varepsilon_{t-1} + \\cdots + \\theta_q\\varepsilon_{t-q} = \\mu + \\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\varepsilon_t \\sim WN(0,\\sigma^2) \\).\n\n**Always weakly stationary** (finite linear combination of WN).\n\n**Invertibility condition:** Roots of \\( \\theta(z) = 1 + \\theta_1 z + \\cdots + \\theta_q z^q = 0 \\) must lie **outside the unit circle**. Ensures the MA process can be written as an infinite AR.\n\n**ACF of MA(q):**\n\\[\n\\rho_k = \\frac{\\sum_{j=0}^{q-k}\\theta_j\\theta_{j+k}}{\\sum_{j=0}^q \\theta_j^2}, \\quad k = 1,\\ldots,q; \\qquad \\rho_k = 0, \\quad k > q\n\\]\n**ACF cuts off after lag \\( q \\)** (key identification fact).\n\n**PACF of MA(q):** Tails off — exponential decay and/or damped sine waves.\n\n*MA(1) example:* \\( \\rho_1 = \\theta_1/(1+\\theta_1^2) \\), \\( \\rho_k = 0 \\) for \\( k \\ge 2 \\).\n\n---\n\n### AR(p) Model\n\n\\[\nY_t = c + \\phi_1 Y_{t-1} + \\cdots + \\phi_p Y_{t-p} + \\varepsilon_t = c + \\phi(B)^{-1}\\varepsilon_t\n\\]\n\n**Stationarity condition:** Roots of \\( \\phi(z) = 1 - \\phi_1 z - \\cdots - \\phi_p z^p = 0 \\) must lie **outside the unit circle**.\n\n*AR(1):* Stationary iff \\( |\\phi_1| < 1 \\). Mean \\( = c/(1-\\phi_1) \\). Variance \\( = \\sigma^2/(1-\\phi_1^2) \\).\n\n**ACF of AR(p):** Tails off — exponential decay or damped sine waves (never cuts off).\n\n**PACF of AR(p):** **Cuts off after lag \\( p \\)** — \\( \\phi_{kk} = 0 \\) for \\( k > p \\).\n\n*AR(1) ACF:* \\( \\rho_k = \\phi_1^k \\). For \\( \\phi_1 > 0 \\): monotone decay; \\( \\phi_1 < 0 \\): alternating decay.\n\n**Yule–Walker equations for AR(p):**\n\\[\n\\rho_k = \\phi_1\\rho_{k-1} + \\phi_2\\rho_{k-2} + \\cdots + \\phi_p\\rho_{k-p}, \\quad k = 1,2,\\ldots,p\n\\]\nIn matrix form: \\( \\mathbf{P}\\boldsymbol{\\phi} = \\boldsymbol{\\rho} \\implies \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}} = \\hat{\\mathbf{P}}^{-1}\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\rho}} \\).\n\n---\n\n### ARMA(p,q) Model\n\n\\[\n\\phi(B)Y_t = \\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t\n\\]\n\n- Stationarity: roots of \\( \\phi(z) = 0 \\) outside unit circle.\n- Invertibility: roots of \\( \\theta(z) = 0 \\) outside unit circle.\n- **Both ACF and PACF tail off** — neither cuts off.\n\n---\n\n### ARIMA(p,d,q) Model\n\nFor non-stationary series requiring \\( d \\) differences to achieve stationarity:\n\\[\n\\phi(B)(1-B)^d Y_t = \\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t\n\\]\n\n- \\( d = 0 \\): stationary series (ARMA).\n- \\( d = 1 \\): random walk type; first difference \\( \\Delta Y_t = Y_t - Y_{t-1} \\) is stationary.\n- \\( d = 2 \\): second difference needed.\n\n**Seasonal ARIMA: SARIMA\\((p,d,q)(P,D,Q)_s\\):**\n\\[\n\\phi(B)\\Phi(B^s)(1-B)^d(1-B^s)^D Y_t = \\theta(B)\\Theta(B^s)\\varepsilon_t\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) = seasonal period.\n\n---\n\n### ACF/PACF Identification Summary\n\n| Model | ACF | PACF |\n|---|---|---|\n| MA(q) | **Cuts off** after lag \\( q \\) | Tails off |\n| AR(p) | Tails off | **Cuts off** after lag \\( p \\) |\n| ARMA(p,q) | Tails off | Tails off |\n| White noise | \\( \\approx 0 \\) for all \\( k > 0 \\) | \\( \\approx 0 \\) for all \\( k > 0 \\) |\n| Random walk | Slow linear decay | Spike at lag 1 only |\n\n---\n\n### Box–Jenkins Methodology (4 Steps)\n\n**Step 1 — Identification:**\n- Plot \\( Y_t \\); if non-stationary, difference \\( d \\) times until ACF decays quickly.\n- Use ADF (Augmented Dickey-Fuller) test for formal unit root testing.\n- Examine sample ACF and PACF to identify tentative \\( p \\) and \\( q \\).\n- Use **AIC / BIC** for model selection among competing candidates:\n\\[\n\\text{AIC} = -2\\ell + 2k, \\qquad \\text{BIC} = -2\\ell + k\\log n\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\ell \\) = log-likelihood, \\( k \\) = number of parameters. Lower is better.\n\n**Step 2 — Estimation:**\n- AR parameters: solve Yule–Walker equations (method of moments) or use conditional MLE.\n- MA and ARMA parameters: unconditional/exact MLE via numerical optimisation (Gauss–Newton).\n\n**Step 3 — Diagnostic Checking:**\n- Residuals \\( \\hat{\\varepsilon}_t \\) should be white noise.\n- **Ljung–Box Q test:** \\( Q = n(n+2)\\sum_{k=1}^m \\hat{\\rho}_k^2/(n-k) \\sim \\chi^2_{m-p-q} \\) under \\( H_0 \\) of white noise residuals.\n- Check residual ACF for spikes; if significant at seasonal lags, add seasonal terms.\n\n**Step 4 — Forecasting:**\nMinimum MSE (conditional expectation) \\( h \\)-step-ahead forecast:\n\\[\n\\hat{Y}_{T+h|T} = E(Y_{T+h}|Y_T, Y_{T-1},\\ldots)\n\\]\nFor ARIMA(1,0,0): \\( \\hat{Y}_{T+h|T} = \\hat{c}/(1-\\hat{\\phi}_1) + \\hat{\\phi}_1^h(Y_T - \\hat{c}/(1-\\hat{\\phi}_1)) \\)\n\nForecast error variance increases with \\( h \\) — construct \\( 95\\% \\) PI as \\( \\hat{Y}_{T+h|T} \\pm 1.96\\hat{\\sigma}_h \\).\n\n> **ISS tip (Descriptive):** The ACF/PACF identification table is a guaranteed question. Memorise: MA(q) → ACF cuts off; AR(p) → PACF cuts off. For ARMA: both tail off. Always state the **invertibility condition** for MA models (roots outside unit circle) alongside the stationarity condition for AR. For Box–Jenkins, write all 4 steps with the Ljung–Box test formula. AIC/BIC for model selection — state BIC penalises more heavily than AIC."
