                {
                    "id": "applied-ts-spectral-estimation",
                    "label": "Large Sample Estimation & Spectral Analysis",
                    "content": "## Large Sample Estimation, Spectral Analysis & Periodogram\n\n### Estimation Under Large Sample Theory\n\n*(Without proofs, as per syllabus.)*\n\nGiven \\( Y_1, Y_2, \\ldots, Y_n \\) from a weakly stationary process with mean \\( \\mu \\), ACVF \\( \\gamma_k \\), ACF \\( \\rho_k \\).\n\n**Sample mean:**\n\\[\n\\bar{Y} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^n Y_t\n\\]\n- Unbiased; consistent.\n- \\( \\text{Var}(\\bar{Y}) \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\left(1-\\dfrac{|k|}{n}\\right)\\gamma_k \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^\\infty \\gamma_k \\) for large \\( n \\).\n\n**Sample ACVF:**\n\\[\n\\hat{\\gamma}_k = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{n-|k|}(Y_t - \\bar{Y})(Y_{t+|k|} - \\bar{Y})\n\\]\n*Note:* Divisor is \\( n \\) (not \\( n-k \\)) to ensure the sample ACVF matrix is **positive semi-definite**.\n\n**Sample ACF:** \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k = \\hat{\\gamma}_k/\\hat{\\gamma}_0 \\)\n\n**Bartlett's theorem (large sample distribution of \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\)):**\nFor a linear process \\( Y_t = \\sum_j \\psi_j \\varepsilon_{t-j} \\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{n}\\hat{\\rho}_k \\xrightarrow{d} N(0, w_{kk})\n\\]\nwhere \\( w_{kk} = \\sum_{j=-\\infty}^\\infty(\\rho_j^2 + \\rho_{j+k}\\rho_{j-k} - 4\\rho_k\\rho_j\\rho_{j-k} + 2\\rho_k^2\\rho_j^2) \\).\n\n*For white noise:* \\( w_{kk} = 1 \\), so \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\stackrel{approx}{\\sim} N(0, 1/n) \\) and significance bands are \\( \\pm 1.96/\\sqrt{n} \\).\n\n**Estimation of ARIMA model parameters:**\n- **AR(p) — Yule–Walker (method of moments):** Substitute \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\) into the Yule–Walker equations \\( \\rho_k = \\sum_{j=1}^p \\phi_j\\rho_{k-j} \\); solve \\( \\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\phi}} = \\hat{P}^{-1}\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\rho}} \\). Consistent; asymptotically normal. Optimal only for pure AR.\n- **MA/ARMA — MLE:** Since \\( \\varepsilon_t \\) is unobservable, Yule–Walker gives a nonlinear system; use conditional or exact MLE via iterative algorithms (Newton–Raphson, Gauss–Newton). MLE is asymptotically efficient.\n- **ARIMA(p,d,q):** Difference \\( d \\) times first; then apply ARMA estimation to the differenced series.\n\n---\n\n### Wold's Decomposition Theorem\n\n**Theorem (Wold, 1938):** Every purely non-deterministic covariance-stationary process \\( \\{Y_t\\} \\) can be written uniquely as:\n\\[\nY_t = \\sum_{j=0}^\\infty \\psi_j\\varepsilon_{t-j} + V_t\n\\]\nwhere:\n- \\( \\psi_0 = 1 \\), \\( \\sum_j \\psi_j^2 < \\infty \\)\n- \\( \\varepsilon_t = Y_t - \\hat{Y}_{t|t-1,t-2,\\ldots} \\) is the **innovation** (one-step-ahead forecast error), \\( \\varepsilon_t \\sim WN(0,\\sigma^2) \\)\n- \\( V_t \\) is a deterministic component (linearly predictable from its own past)\n\n**Significance:** Justifies the use of ARMA models — every stationary process has a moving average representation. This underpins the theoretical validity of Box–Jenkins.\n\n---\n\n### Spectral Analysis\n\nTime domain (ACF/PACF) and frequency domain (spectrum) are **dual representations** of the same process — related by the Fourier transform.\n\n**Spectral density function:**\nFor a stationary process with \\( \\sum_k |\\gamma_k| < \\infty \\), the spectral density is:\n\\[\nf(\\omega) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\gamma_k e^{-i\\omega k} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\left(\\gamma_0 + 2\\sum_{k=1}^\\infty \\gamma_k \\cos(\\omega k)\\right)\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\omega \\in [-\\pi, \\pi] \\) is the angular frequency. \\( f(\\omega) \\ge 0 \\) for all \\( \\omega \\).\n\n**Inverse relationship (inversion formula):**\n\\[\n\\gamma_k = \\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} e^{i\\omega k} f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega\n\\]\nSetting \\( k = 0 \\): \\( \\gamma_0 = \\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\omega)\\,d\\omega \\) — **total variance equals area under spectral density**.\n\nThis Fourier transform pair is the fundamental duality: **ACF ↔ Spectral density** (via Wiener–Khintchine theorem).\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- A peak in \\( f(\\omega) \\) at frequency \\( \\omega_0 \\) means the process has strong periodic behaviour with period \\( 2\\pi/\\omega_0 \\).\n- White noise: \\( f(\\omega) = \\sigma^2/(2\\pi) \\) — flat spectrum (all frequencies equally present).\n- AR(1) with \\( \\phi > 0 \\): spectrum peaks at \\( \\omega = 0 \\) (low frequency/long cycles dominate).\n- Seasonal process: spikes at seasonal frequency \\( \\omega = 2\\pi/s \\).\n\n---\n\n### Periodogram Analysis\n\nThe **periodogram** is the sample (non-parametric) estimator of the spectral density, computed via the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT).\n\n**DFT of the series:**\n\\[\nd(\\omega_j) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\sum_{t=1}^n Y_t e^{-i\\omega_j t}, \\qquad \\omega_j = \\frac{2\\pi j}{n}, \\quad j = 0, 1, \\ldots, \\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor\n\\]\n\n**Periodogram:**\n\\[\nI(\\omega_j) = |d(\\omega_j)|^2 = \\frac{1}{n}\\left|\\sum_{t=1}^n Y_t e^{-i\\omega_j t}\\right|^2\n\\]\n\n**Relationship to sample ACVF:**\n\\[\nI(\\omega_j) = \\sum_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1}\\hat{\\gamma}_k e^{-i\\omega_j k}\n\\]\nThe periodogram is the Fourier transform of the sample ACVF — confirming the **Correlogram ↔ Periodogram duality**.\n\n**Critical property:** The periodogram is an **inconsistent** estimator of \\( f(\\omega) \\):\n\\[\nE[I(\\omega_j)] \\approx f(\\omega_j) \\quad \\text{(asymptotically unbiased)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Var}[I(\\omega_j)] \\not\\to 0 \\quad \\text{as } n \\to \\infty\n\\]\nThe variance does not decrease with sample size — periodogram values at different frequencies are asymptotically independent but each has variance \\( \\approx f(\\omega)^2 \\).\n\n**Smoothed Periodogram (consistent estimator):**\nTo get a consistent estimate, apply a **spectral window** \\( \\{w_s\\} \\) (kernel smoother):\n\\[\n\\hat{f}(\\omega) = \\sum_s w_s I(\\omega + \\omega_s)\n\\]\nCommon windows: Bartlett, Parzen, Daniell, Tukey–Hanning. As bandwidth \\( \\to 0 \\) and number of averaged periodogram ordinates \\( \\to \\infty \\) (slowly), \\( \\hat{f}(\\omega) \\xrightarrow{p} f(\\omega) \\).\n\n**Computations using FFT:** The DFT of \\( n \\) points can be computed in \\( O(n\\log n) \\) operations using the Fast Fourier Transform (Cooley–Tukey algorithm) rather than \\( O(n^2) \\) for direct computation.\n\n---\n\n### Correlogram vs Periodogram\n\n| Feature | Correlogram | Periodogram |\n|---|---|---|\n| Domain | Time domain | Frequency domain |\n| Plots | \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\) vs lag \\( k \\) | \\( I(\\omega_j) \\) vs frequency \\( \\omega_j \\) |\n| Use | Identify ARMA order; test white noise | Detect hidden periodicities; estimate spectrum |\n| Consistency | \\( \\hat{\\rho}_k \\xrightarrow{p} \\rho_k \\) ✓ | \\( I(\\omega_j) \\) is inconsistent ✗ (raw) |\n| Relationship | Fourier transform pair (Wiener–Khintchine) | |\n\n> **ISS tip (Descriptive):** Three things examiners always check in spectral analysis questions: (1) State the inversion formula \\( \\gamma_k = \\int e^{i\\omega k}f(\\omega)d\\omega \\) and set \\( k=0 \\) to get \\( \\gamma_0 = \\int f(\\omega)d\\omega \\) (total variance). (2) State that the raw periodogram is **asymptotically unbiased but inconsistent** — variance does not go to zero. (3) Smoothing is required for a consistent spectral estimator. The Correlogram and Periodogram are Fourier transform duals — state the Wiener–Khintchine theorem explicitly. For Wold's decomposition: every stationary process = MA(∞) + deterministic part — this justifies Box–Jenkins."
                }
